# Line endings.
#
# The Windows Git installer sets core.autocrlf=true in the *system* gitconfig,
# so a clone or checkout on Windows would turn these into CRLF and break them:
#
#   build.sh / install.sh   ->  bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M
#   Makefile                ->  missing separator
#
# Pinning eol=lf here wins over whatever the person cloning has configured, on
# every platform.
*.sh      text eol=lf
Makefile  text eol=lf

# Everything else: normalise to LF in the repository, check out natively.
* text=auto

# Images and other binary assets. Never let git guess, and never let it run
# these through the line-ending filter.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.pdf   binary

# Compiled output. .gitignore already keeps these out of the repository, but
# the rules below are the belt to that pair of braces: if one is ever tracked
# on purpose, git must not push 8 MB of machine code through a text filter.
*.exe   binary
*.dll   binary
*.so    binary
*.dylib binary
dist/** binary
bin/**  binary
